  "prompt_text": "The figure compares how oxygen-related redox changes are represented in three oxide crystal families by moving from bulk structures to exposed surfaces or phase transitions.\n\nAt the top, fluorite CeO2 is shown transforming into a CeO2(110) surface, where oxidation and reduction are linked to two different oxygen-vacancy arrangements. The key message is that removing or restoring oxygen at the surface can create either an in-plane vacancy or a split vacancy, highlighting distinct local defect geometries on ceria surfaces.\n\nIn the middle, perovskite LaFeO3 is shown as a bulk crystal and then as a LaFeO3(010) surface. Here, reduction and oxidation are associated with changes around a surface FeO6 octahedral environment, suggesting that the redox response is described mainly by the local octahedral coordination at the surface rather than by multiple vacancy types.\n\nAt the bottom, iron oxides are presented as a sequence of bulk phases: FeO, Fe3O4, and Fe2O3. Bidirectional arrows indicate that reduction and oxidation drive reversible structural conversion among these phases. Overall, the figure emphasizes that redox in oxides can proceed either through local surface oxygen-vacancy rearrangements, as in ceria and LaFeO3, or through larger crystallographic phase changes, as in iron oxides.",
